> Thank you for this amazing new plugin!
> 
> mousescrolling is very weird and slow with animate though. Is there any way to disable animate specifically for mouse scrolling? Since that is already smooth anyway, without animate or neoscroll

It is slow, because scrolling with many wheel turns result into many consecutive `WinScrolled` events. Each new one takes over the previous one *at the current view state*. As far as I researched, there is no way of knowing inside `WinScrolled` event which action triggered it. That is why it has a special mention [in help file](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/093211078a5d0764c8beb489f9e09e4a4d962de9/doc/mini-animate.txt#L262-L277).

There are several ways to overcome this:
- Use high value for vertical mouse scrolling. Like `set mousescroll=ver:25,hor:6`. This will need one mouse wheel turn to go far enough. This is a method I adopted.
- Use `set mousescroll=ver:1,hor:6`. This will need aggressive mouse turns to go far enough.
- Use predicate for scrolling, such that it won't animate if total scroll is at or lower mouse scroll. Assuming default `mousescroll`:
```lua
local animate = require('mini.animate')
animate.setup({
  scroll = {
    subscroll = animate.gen_subscroll.equal({ predicate = function(total_scroll) return total_scroll > 3 end }),
  },
})
```
- Use different timing for small total scroll. It won't completely solve the problem (there will be an undershoot in case of aggressive mouse wheel turning), but it is a possible solution also:
```lua
local animate = require('mini.animate')
animate.setup({
  scroll = {
    timing = function(_, n) return math.min(5, 250 / n) end
  },
})
```

The code below can detect when a `WinScrolled` event was due to mouse scroll.

If you'd incorporate it in mini, you would not need the `schedule()` inside the `WinScrolled` handler. Just always set it to `false`

```lua
  local scrolled = false
  for _, scroll in ipairs({ "Up", "Down" }) do
    local key = "<ScrollWheel" .. scroll .. ">"
    vim.keymap.set("", key, function()
      scrolled = true
      return key
    end, { noremap = true, expr = true })
  end
  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("WinScrolled", {
    callback = function()
      vim.schedule(function()
        scrolled = false
      end)
    end,
  })

  local animate = require("mini.animate")
  animate.setup({
    scroll = {
      enabled = false,
      timing = animate.gen_timing.linear({ duration = 150, unit = "total" }),
      subscroll = animate.gen_subscroll.equal({
        predicate = function(total_scroll)
          if scrolled then
            return false
          end
          return total_scroll > 1
        end,
      }),
    },
  })
```

> Thanks for this great work! Is it possible to have the same animation for horizontal jumps on the same line?

Yes. When animation is done depends on output of `MiniAnimate.config.cursor.path`: if it is an empty or `nil`, no animation is done. Write now animation doesn't happen cursor is moved within neighbor lines (reduces flicker). Here is one way to animate on any cursor move:

```lua
local animate = require('mini.animate')
animate.setup({
  cursor = {
    -- Animate with shortest line for any cursor move
    path = animate.gen_path.line({
      predicate = function() return true end,
    }),
  }
})
```

As you are not the first one asking this, I updated documentation to have [this example](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/17304ee11d0d80ba76c0efd3cf0dbe633278ef11/doc/mini-animate.txt#L228-L239). So thanks for the question!

@austinliuigi, I just force-pushed updates into same [animate_scroll-proportional-cursor](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/tree/animate_scroll-proportional-cursor) branch. It should deal with the main (first) issue I mentioned: cursor tracking for scroll animation should be up to date now. Ideally, there should be no more visual glitches when cursor has moved after recent scroll.

There is also some neat update for intermediate cursor placing: it now can place it outside of line which results into slightly smoother animations.

Would you mind checking it out? If everything goes smooth, I plan merging it in `main` tomorrow.

> Is there a good way to exclude certain filetypes from this resizing? Things like a filetree or a symbol outline get resized.

The problem seems to be that some plugins that open normal windows call `wincmd =` in certain point of their window creation which leads to an equal resize. This causes the `winwidth` / `winheight` options to take effect.

Generally speaking, the proper way to make a window not resize width is by setting `winfixwidth` option for that particular window. It then should have the current width untouched. The tricky part is to set it automatically for appropriate windows, which is plugin-spescific.

For 'neo-tree.nvim' adding this configuration seems to do the trick:

```lua
require('neo-tree').setup({
  event_handlers = {
    {
      event = 'neo_tree_window_after_open',
      handler = function(data)
        local winid = data.winid
        vim.api.nvim_win_set_option(winid, 'winfixwidth', true)
        vim.api.nvim_win_set_width(winid, 40)
      end,
    }
  }
})
```

[Kooha-2023-01-31-15-21-02.webm](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/684721/215803564-4738cdc2-92b3-4660-b6c3-e11582c89c03.webm)


The video demonstrates a memory leak. Neovim version is `0.9.0`. Disabling `mini.animate` fixes the issue. My config is as follows:

```lua
"echasnovski/mini.animate",
event = "VeryLazy",
config = function()
  local is_not_single_window = function(win_id)
    local tabpage_id = vim.api.nvim_win_get_tabpage(win_id)
    return #vim.api.nvim_tabpage_list_wins(tabpage_id) > 1
  end

  local animate = require("mini.animate")
  animate.setup({
    scroll = {
      timing = animate.gen_timing.linear({ duration = 150, unit = "total" }),
      subscroll = animate.gen_subscroll.equal({ max_output_steps = 120 }),
    },
    open = {
      winconfig = animate.gen_winconfig.wipe({
        predicate = is_not_single_window,
        direction = "from_edge",
      }),
    },
    close = {
      winconfig = animate.gen_winconfig.wipe({
        predicate = is_not_single_window,
        direction = "to_edge",
      }),
    },
  })
end
```
